// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the English Privacy page > is word for word as before with sign_in=off notices=off streaks=off spanish=off 1`] = `"PrivacyLast updated: October 29, 2026The short versionYou can read and listen with no account. The site does not ask for your name or your email address.The site shows no advertising, and it sells no data.It counts which pages and features readers use, with no cookie and no identifier.It records what readers search for, with no link to the reader.Questions: privacy@urantiahub.com.Who we areAdams Technologies LLC runs UrantiaHub. On this page, “we” means Adams Technologies LLC. UrantiaHub is an independent project. It is not affiliated with Urantia Foundation.What the site collects, and whyPage analyticsWhen you open a page or use a feature, the site sends an event to PostHog, our analytics provider. An event holds the page address and a label such as a paper number. It holds no text that you read. The address of a search page is sent without the words of the search. The site sets no analytics cookie and keeps no analytics identifier in your browser. PostHog processes your IP address when it receives a request from your browser.We use this to see which parts of the site help readers, and to find faults.Ask an assistantA paper offers “Ask an assistant”. When you use it, your browser opens the site of the assistant that you chose (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, or Grok) with your question and the paragraphs that it is about. UrantiaHub does not receive the question or the answer, and it pays no assistant. The company of that assistant handles both under its own terms. The site counts that the feature was used, never the words.SearchesWhen you search, the site records the words of the search and how many results it found, so that we can see what readers look for. The record has no link to you. Our server sends it, not your browser, so it holds no IP address, no page that you opened, and nothing about your device.To find results, our server sends the words of the search to the content service that holds the text. For the related passages, that service sends the words to OpenAI, which compares their meaning with the text. Neither one receives your IP address or anything else about you.Request logsAs for each website, our hosting provider keeps a log of each request for a limited time, to keep the site safe and to find faults. A log line holds your IP address and the address of the page. The address of a search page holds the words of the search.What stays in your browserThe site keeps these things in your browser. They do not leave your device.Your theme, so the page opens light or dark, as you chose.Your text size, so a paper opens at the size that you chose.The last place that you read, so the contents page can offer to continue there.The paragraph where the voice stopped, so the paper can offer to continue listening there.The last page that you read in each world text, so the page of that work can offer to continue there.The papers, if you turn on Read offline, so you can read with no connection; turn it off, and they leave the device.The assistant that you chose, if you use Ask an assistant, so the next question opens in the same one.If you added the site to your home screen: that it opened in this session, so it opens at your last place only when it starts; the mark goes when the app closes.Your last five searches, so you can run one again; the Clear control on the search page removes them.You can remove each of them when you clear the site data in your browser.Who handles data for usThese companies do work for the site. Each one receives only what its work needs.CompanyWhat it doesWhat it receivesVercelPolicyHosts the site.Each request to the site: your IP address, the page address, the time, and your browser type.PostHogPolicyAnalytics: which pages and features readers use.An event for a page or an action, with your IP address. No cookie and no identifier. Also the search records, which have no link to you.CloudflarePolicyServes the audio files, and runs the content service that holds the text.Your IP address when your browser loads an audio file. The words of a search, from our server.OpenAIPolicyCompares the meaning of a search with the text, for the related passages.The words of a search, from the content service. Nothing about you.We do not sell data, and we do not share it for advertising.Your choicesIf your browser sends a Do Not Track or a Global Privacy Control signal, the site sends no analytics event and makes no record of your searches in its analytics.You can clear the site data in your browser at any time. The site still works.You can ask what we hold, or ask us to remove it: privacy@urantiahub.com. With no account, we hold nothing under your name, so tell us what you look for.Changes to this pageThe site will grow: accounts, saved places, and notes are planned. When this page changes, the date at the top changes, and this page says what is new.ContactSend a question about your privacy to privacy@urantiahub.com."`;

exports[`the English Privacy page > is word for word as before with sign_in=off notices=off streaks=off spanish=on 1`] = `"PrivacyLast updated: October 29, 2026The short versionYou can read and listen with no account. The site does not ask for your name or your email address.The site shows no advertising, and it sells no data.It counts which pages and features readers use, with no cookie and no identifier.It records what readers search for, with no link to the reader.Questions: privacy@urantiahub.com.Who we areAdams Technologies LLC runs UrantiaHub. On this page, “we” means Adams Technologies LLC. UrantiaHub is an independent project. It is not affiliated with Urantia Foundation.What the site collects, and whyPage analyticsWhen you open a page or use a feature, the site sends an event to PostHog, our analytics provider. An event holds the page address and a label such as a paper number. It holds no text that you read. The address of a search page is sent without the words of the search. The site sets no analytics cookie and keeps no analytics identifier in your browser. PostHog processes your IP address when it receives a request from your browser.We use this to see which parts of the site help readers, and to find faults.Ask an assistantA paper offers “Ask an assistant”. When you use it, your browser opens the site of the assistant that you chose (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, or Grok) with your question and the paragraphs that it is about. UrantiaHub does not receive the question or the answer, and it pays no assistant. The company of that assistant handles both under its own terms. The site counts that the feature was used, never the words.SearchesWhen you search, the site records the words of the search and how many results it found, so that we can see what readers look for. The record has no link to you. Our server sends it, not your browser, so it holds no IP address, no page that you opened, and nothing about your device.To find results, our server sends the words of the search to the content service that holds the text. For the related passages, that service sends the words to OpenAI, which compares their meaning with the text. Neither one receives your IP address or anything else about you.Request logsAs for each website, our hosting provider keeps a log of each request for a limited time, to keep the site safe and to find faults. A log line holds your IP address and the address of the page. The address of a search page holds the words of the search.What stays in your browserThe site keeps these things in your browser. They do not leave your device.Your theme, so the page opens light or dark, as you chose.Your text size, so a paper opens at the size that you chose.The last place that you read, so the contents page can offer to continue there.The paragraph where the voice stopped, so the paper can offer to continue listening there.The last page that you read in each world text, so the page of that work can offer to continue there.The papers, if you turn on Read offline, so you can read with no connection; turn it off, and they leave the device.The assistant that you chose, if you use Ask an assistant, so the next question opens in the same one.If you added the site to your home screen: that it opened in this session, so it opens at your last place only when it starts; the mark goes when the app closes.Your last five searches, so you can run one again; the Clear control on the search page removes them.The language that you chose to read in, and when you chose it, so the site does not offer the other language again, and the newest choice wins on each of your devices.That this tab already opened a page of the site, so only the first page of a visit opens in your language.You can remove each of them when you clear the site data in your browser.Who handles data for usThese companies do work for the site. Each one receives only what its work needs.CompanyWhat it doesWhat it receivesVercelPolicyHosts the site.Each request to the site: your IP address, the page address, the time, and your browser type.PostHogPolicyAnalytics: which pages and features readers use.An event for a page or an action, with your IP address. No cookie and no identifier. Also the search records, which have no link to you.CloudflarePolicyServes the audio files, and runs the content service that holds the text.Your IP address when your browser loads an audio file. The words of a search, from our server.OpenAIPolicyCompares the meaning of a search with the text, for the related passages.The words of a search, from the content service. Nothing about you.We do not sell data, and we do not share it for advertising.Your choicesIf your browser sends a Do Not Track or a Global Privacy Control signal, the site sends no analytics event and makes no record of your searches in its analytics.You can clear the site data in your browser at any time. The site still works.You can ask what we hold, or ask us to remove it: privacy@urantiahub.com. With no account, we hold nothing under your name, so tell us what you look for.Changes to this pageThe site will grow: accounts, saved places, and notes are planned. When this page changes, the date at the top changes, and this page says what is new.ContactSend a question about your privacy to privacy@urantiahub.com."`;

