, Gemini, or Grok) with your question and the paragraphs that it is about. UrantiaHub does not receive the question or the answer, and it pays no assistant. The company of that assistant handles both under its own terms. The site counts that the feature was used, never the words.SearchesWhen you search, the site records the words of the search and how many results it found, so that we can see what readers look for. The record has no link to you. Our server sends it, not your browser, so it holds no IP address, no page that you opened, and nothing about your device.To find results, our server sends the words of the search to the content service that holds the text. For the related passages, that service sends the words to OpenAI, which compares their meaning with the text. Neither one receives your IP address or anything else about you.Request logsAs for each website, our hosting provider keeps a log of each request for a limited time, to keep the site safe and to find faults. A log line holds your IP address and the address of the page. The address of a search page holds the words of the search.If you sign inYou can sign in with a UrantiaHub account. It is optional, and nothing on the site needs it for reading or listening. If you sign in, the account holds these things, so that they are the same on each of your devices:Your email address, and your name if your account has one.The last place that you read.Which paragraphs you read, and when.The paragraphs that you saved.Your notes.The language that you chose to read in, and when you chose it.One cookie holds your sign-in. It is encrypted, and a script on the page cannot read it. A second cookie says only that you are signed in, so that the page can show the right controls. We use neither one for analytics or advertising. While you are signed in, each analytics event carries a code that stands for your account, as the section on page analytics says.A few more cookies serve the sign-in itself: one holds the start of a sign-in and the language of this browser for a few minutes, one remembers that you signed out, so that the next sign-in asks you again, and one says for a minute that a sign-in did not finish. The browser also keeps a mark that says which account the place in this browser belongs to.When you sign out on a device, the last place that you read leaves that browser. Your account still holds it.On accounts.urantiahub.com you can see each app that has access to your account, remove one, or delete the account. When you delete the account, we delete what it holds.What stays in your browserThe site keeps these things in your browser. If you sign in, the place, the language, and the paragraphs that you read also go to your account. The theme and the text size never leave the device. For each other reader, nothing of this leaves the device.Your theme, so the page opens light or dark, as you chose.Your text size, so a paper opens at the size that you chose.The last place that you read, so the contents page can offer to continue there.The paragraph where the voice stopped, so the paper can offer to continue listening there.The last page that you read in each world text, so the page of that work can offer to continue there.The papers, if you turn on Read offline, so you can read with no connection; turn it off, and they leave the device.The assistant that you chose, if you use Ask an assistant, so the next question opens in the same one.If you added the site to your home screen: that it opened in this session, so it opens at your last place only when it starts; the mark goes when the app closes.Your last five searches, so you can run one again; the Clear control on the search page removes them.The language that you chose to read in, and when you chose it, so the site does not offer the other language again, and the newest choice wins on each of your devices.That this tab already opened a page of the site, so only the first page of a visit opens in your language.You can remove each of them when you clear the site data in your browser.Who handles data for usThese companies do work for the site. Each one receives only what its work needs.CompanyWhat it doesWhat it receivesVercelPolicyHosts the site.Each request to the site: your IP address, the page address, the time, and your browser type.PostHogPolicyAnalytics: which pages and features readers use.An event for a page or an action, with your IP address. No cookie. While you are signed in, also a code that stands for your account: not your email address and not your name. Also the search records, which have no link to you.CloudflarePolicyServes the audio files, and runs the content service that holds the text.Your IP address when your browser loads an audio file. The words of a search, from our server.OpenAIPolicyCompares the meaning of a search with the text, for the related passages.The words of a search, from the content service. Nothing about you.SupabasePolicyHolds the accounts, and what an account keeps.Your email address, your name and picture if you sign in with Google, the last place that you read, which paragraphs you read, the paragraphs that you saved, your notes, and the language that you chose.ResendPolicySends the email with the sign-in code.Your email address and the code.GooglePolicySigns you in, only if you choose “Continue with Google”.That you signed in to UrantiaHub. Google gives us your email address, your name, and your picture.We do not sell data, and we do not share it for advertising.Your choicesIf your browser sends a Do Not Track or a Global Privacy Control signal, the site sends no analytics event and makes no record of your searches in its analytics.You can clear the site data in your browser at any time. The site still works.You can ask what we hold, or ask us to remove it: privacy@urantiahub.com. With no account, we hold nothing under your name, so tell us what you look for.Changes to this pageNew on this date: this page no longer calls saved paragraphs and notes planned, because you can use them now. It also corrects one line about the sign-in cookies: while you are signed in, analytics events carry a code that stands for your account. When this page changes, the date at the top changes, and this page says what is new.ContactSend a question about your privacy to privacy@urantiahub.com."`;

