ok) with your question and the paragraphs that it is about. UrantiaHub does not receive the question or the answer, and it pays no assistant. The company of that assistant handles both under its own terms. The site counts that the feature was used, never the words.SearchesWhen you search, the site records the words of the search and how many results it found, so that we can see what readers look for. The record has no link to you. Our server sends it, not your browser, so it holds no IP address, no page that you opened, and nothing about your device.To find results, our server sends the words of the search to the content service that holds the text. For the related passages, that service sends the words to OpenAI, which compares their meaning with the text. Neither one receives your IP address or anything else about you.Request logsAs for each website, our hosting provider keeps a log of each request for a limited time, to keep the site safe and to find faults. A log line holds your IP address and the address of the page. The address of a search page holds the words of the search.If you sign inYou can sign in with a UrantiaHub account. It is optional, and nothing on the site needs it for reading or listening. If you sign in, the account holds these things, so that they are the same on each of your devices:Your email address, and your name if your account has one.The last place that you read.Which paragraphs you read, and when.The paragraphs that you saved.Your notes.One cookie holds your sign-in. It is encrypted, and a script on the page cannot read it. A second cookie says only that you are signed in, so that the page can show the right controls. We use neither one for analytics or advertising. While you are signed in, each analytics event carries a code that stands for your account, as the section on page analytics says.A few more cookies serve the sign-in itself: one holds the start of a sign-in for a few minutes, one remembers that you signed out, so that the next sign-in asks you again, and one says for a minute that a sign-in did not finish. The browser also keeps a mark that says which account the place in this browser belongs to.When you sign out on a device, the last place that you read leaves that browser. Your account still holds it.On accounts.urantiahub.com you can see each app that has access to your account, remove one, or delete the account. When you delete the account, we delete what it holds.If you keep a streakA signed-in reader can keep a streak: a count of the days in a row with a finished section. It is off until you say yes. If you keep one, the account also holds these things:If you keep a streak: each section that you finished, the day, and if you read it or listened to it.Your streak settings: on or off, the sections a day, your time zone, the day that you started, and your longest streak.If you keep a streak: the pages of the world texts that you finished, for the marks.You can turn the streak off on your page. What the account holds for it stays, so a streak that you turn on again has its count. When you delete the account, we delete all of this.What stays in your browserThe site keeps these things in your browser. If you sign in, the place and the paragraphs that you read also go to your account. The theme and the text size never leave the device. For each other reader, nothing of this leaves the device.Your theme, so the page opens light or dark, as you chose.Your text size, so a paper opens at the size that you chose.The last place that you read, so the contents page can offer to continue there.The paragraph where the voice stopped, so the paper can offer to continue listening there.The last page that you read in each world text, so the page of that work can offer to continue there.The papers, if you turn on Read offline, so you can read with no connection; turn it off, and they leave the device.The assistant that you chose, if you use Ask an assistant, so the next question opens in the same one.If you added the site to your home screen: that it opened in this session, so it opens at your last place only when it starts; the mark goes when the app closes.Your last five searches, so you can run one again; the Clear control on the search page removes them.The paragraphs that you read or heard today, while you are signed in, so the site can tell when you finish a section for the streak; the next day replaces them, a sign-out removes them, and only a finished section goes to your account.The passages that you read on a page of a world text, while you are signed in and keep a streak, so the site can tell when you finish the page for the marks; a finished page leaves them, a sign-out removes them, and only the finished page goes to your account.You can remove each of them when you clear the site data in your browser.Who handles data for usThese companies do work for the site. Each one receives only what its work needs.CompanyWhat it doesWhat it receivesVercelPolicyHosts the site.Each request to the site: your IP address, the page address, the time, and your browser type.PostHogPolicyAnalytics: which pages and features readers use.An event for a page or an action, with your IP address. No cookie. While you are signed in, also a code that stands for your account: not your email address and not your name. Also the search records, which have no link to you.CloudflarePolicyServes the audio files, and runs the content service that holds the text.Your IP address when your browser loads an audio file. The words of a search, from our server.OpenAIPolicyCompares the meaning of a search with the text, for the related passages.The words of a search, from the content service. Nothing about you.SupabasePolicyHolds the accounts, and what an account keeps.Your email address, your name and picture if you sign in with Google, the last place that you read, which paragraphs you read, the paragraphs that you saved, your notes, the sections that you finished each day, the pages of the world texts that you finished, your streak settings.ResendPolicySends the email with the sign-in code.Your email address and the code.GooglePolicySigns you in, only if you choose “Continue with Google”.That you signed in to UrantiaHub. Google gives us your email address, your name, and your picture.We do not sell data, and we do not share it for advertising.Your choicesIf your browser sends a Do Not Track or a Global Privacy Control signal, the site sends no analytics event and makes no record of your searches in its analytics.You can clear the site data in your browser at any time. The site still works.You can ask what we hold, or ask us to remove it: privacy@urantiahub.com. With no account, we hold nothing under your name, so tell us what you look for.Changes to this pageNew on this date: this page no longer calls saved paragraphs and notes planned, because you can use them now. It also corrects one line about the sign-in cookies: while you are signed in, analytics events carry a code that stands for your account. When this page changes, the date at the top changes, and this page says what is new.ContactSend a question about your privacy to privacy@urantiahub.com."`;

exports[`the English Privacy page > is word for word as before with sign_in=on notices=off streaks=on spanish=on 1`] = `"PrivacyLast updated: October 29, 2026The short versionYou can read and listen with no account. The site does not ask for your name or your email address.An account is optional. With one, your place, your language, your saved paragraphs, and your notes follow you to each device. The theme and the text size stay on each device.The site shows no advertising, and it sells no data.It counts which pages and features readers use, with no cookie. If you sign in, the counts carry a code that stands for your account, not your email address or your name.It records what readers search for, with no link to the reader.Questions: privacy@urantiahub.com.Who we areAdams Technologies LLC runs UrantiaHub. On this page, “we” means Adams Technologies LLC. UrantiaHub is an independent project. It is not affiliated with Urantia Foundation.What the site collects, and whyPage analyticsWhen you open a page or use a feature, the site sends an event to PostHog, our analytics provider. An event holds the page address and a label such as a paper number. It holds no text that you read. The address of a search page is sent without the words of the search. The site sets no analytics cookie and keeps no analytics identifier in your browser. PostHog processes your IP address when it receives a request from your browser.While you are signed in, each event also carries a code that stands for your account. With it we see how readers with an account use the site over time. The code is not your email address, your name, or the number of your account, and PostHog receives none of those. After you sign out, the events carry no code. The records of searches never carry it.We use this to see which parts of the site help readers, and to find faults.Ask an assistantA paper offers “Ask an assistant”. When you use it, your browser opens the site of the assistant that you chose (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, or Grok) with your question and the paragraphs that it is about. UrantiaHub does not receive the question or the answer, and it pays no assistant. The company of that assistant handles both under its own terms. The site counts that the feature was used, never the words.SearchesWhen you search, the site records the words of the search and how many results it found, so that we can see what readers look for. The record has no link to you. Our server sends it, not your browser, so it holds no IP address, no page that you opened, and nothing about your device.To find results, our server sends the words of the search to the content service that holds the text. For the related passages, that service sends the words to OpenAI, which compares their meaning with the text. Neither one receives your IP address or anything else about you.Request logsAs for each website, our hosting provider keeps a log of each request for a limited time, to keep the site safe and to find faults. A log line holds your IP address and the address of the page. The address of a search page holds the words of the search.If you sign inYou can sign in with a UrantiaHub account. It is optional, and nothing on the site needs it for reading or listening. If you sign in, the account holds these things, so that they are the same on each of your devices:Your email address, and your name if your account has one.The last place that you read.Which paragraphs you read, and when.The paragraphs that you saved.Your notes.The language that you chose to read in, and when you chose it.One cookie holds your sign-in. It is encrypted, and a script on the page cannot read it. A second cookie says only that you are signed in, so that the page can show the right controls. We use neither one for analytics or advertising. While you are signed in, each analytics event carries a code that stands for your account, as the section on page analytics says.A few more cookies serve the sign-in itself: one holds the start of a sign-in and the language of this browser for a few minutes, one remembers that you signed out, so that the next sign-in asks you again, and one says for a minute that a sign-in did not finish. The browser also keeps a mark that says which account the place in this browser belongs to.When you sign out on a device, the last place that you read leaves that browser. Your account still holds it.On accounts.urantiahub.com you can see each app that has access to your account, remove one, or delete the account. When you delete the account, we delete what it holds.If you keep a streakA signed-in reader can keep a streak: a count of the days in a row with a finished section. It is off until you say yes. If you keep one, the account also holds these things:If you keep a streak: each section that you finished, the day, and if you read it or listened to it.Your streak settings: on or off, the sections a day, your time zone, the day that you started, and your longest streak.If you keep a streak: the pages of the world texts that you finished, for the marks.You can turn the streak off on your page. What the account holds for it stays, so a streak that you turn on again has its count. When you delete the account, we delete all of this.What stays in your browserThe site keeps these things in your browser. If you sign in, the place, the language, and the paragraphs that you read also go to your account. The theme and the text size never leave the device. For each other reader, nothing of this leaves the device.Your theme, so the page opens light or dark, as you chose.Your text size, so a paper opens at the size that you chose.The last place that you read, so the contents page can offer to continue there.The paragraph where the voice stopped, so the paper can offer to continue listening there.The last page that you read in each world text, so the page of that work can offer to continue there.The papers, if you turn on Read offline, so you can read with no connection; turn it off, and they leave the device.The assistant that you chose, if you use Ask an assistant, so the next question opens in the same one.If you added the site to your home screen: that it opened in this session, so it opens at your last place only when it starts; the mark goes when the app closes.Your last five searches, so you can run one again; the Clear control on the search page removes them.The paragraphs that you read or heard today, while you are signed in, so the site can tell when you finish a section for the streak; the next day replaces them, a sign-out removes them, and only a finished section goes to your account.The passages that you read on a page of a world text, while you are signed in and keep a streak, so the site can tell when you finish the page for the marks; a finished page leaves them, a sign-out removes them, and only the finished page goes to your account.The language that you chose to read in, and when you chose it, so the site does not offer the other language again, and the newest choice wins on each of your devices.That this tab already opened a page of the site, so only the first page of a visit opens in your language.You can remove each of them when you clear the site data in your browser.Who handles data for usThese companies do work for the site. Each one receives only what its work needs.CompanyWhat it doesWhat it receivesVercelPolicyHosts the site.Each request to the site: your IP address, the page address, the time, and your browser type.PostHogPolicyAnalytics: which pages and features readers use.An event for a page or an action, with your IP address. No cookie. While you are signed in, also a code that stands for your account: not your email address and not your name. Also the search records, which have no link to you.CloudflarePolicyServes the audio files, and runs the content service that holds the text.Your IP address when your browser loads an audio file. The words of a search, from our server.OpenAIPolicyCompares the meaning of a search with the text, for the related passages.The words of a search, from the content service. Nothing about you.SupabasePolicyHolds the accounts, and what an account keeps.Your email address, your name and picture if you sign in with Google, the last place that you read, which paragraphs you read, the paragraphs that you saved, your notes, the sections that you finished each day, the pages of the world texts that you finished, your streak settings, and the language that you chose.ResendPolicySends the email with the sign-in code.Your email address and the code.GooglePolicySigns you in, only if you choose “Continue with Google”.That you signed in to UrantiaHub. Google gives us your email address, your name, and your picture.We do not sell data, and we do not share it for advertising.Your choicesIf your browser sends a Do Not Track or a Global Privacy Control signal, the site sends no analytics event and makes no record of your searches in its analytics.You can clear the site data in your browser at any time. The site still works.You can ask what we hold, or ask us to remove it: privacy@urantiahub.com. With no account, we hold nothing under your name, so tell us what you look for.Changes to this pageNew on this date: this page no longer calls saved paragraphs and notes planned, because you can use them now. It also corrects one line about the sign-in cookies: while you are signed in, analytics events carry a code that stands for your account. When this page changes, the date at the top changes, and this page says what is new.ContactSend a question about your privacy to privacy@urantiahub.com."`;

exports[`the English Privacy page > is word for word as before with sign_in=on notices=on streaks=off spanish=off 1`] = `"PrivacyLast updated: October 29, 2026The short versionYou can read and listen with no account. The site does not ask for your name or your email address.An account is optional. With one, your place, your saved paragraphs, and your notes follow you to each device. The theme and the text size stay on each device.The site shows no advertising, and it sells no data.It counts which pages and features readers use, with no cookie. If you sign in, the counts carry a code that stands for your account, not your email address or your name.It records what readers search for, with no link to the reader.Questions: privacy@urantiahub.com.Who we areAdams Technologies LLC runs UrantiaHub. On this page, “we” means Adams Technologies LLC. UrantiaHub is an independent project. It is not affiliated with Urantia Foundation.What the site collects, and whyPage analyticsWhen you open a page or use a feature, the site sends an event to PostHog, our analytics provider. An event holds the page address and a label such as a paper number. It holds no text that you read. The address of a search page is sent without the words of the search. The site sets no analytics cookie and keeps no analytics identifier in your browser. PostHog processes your IP address when it receives a request from your browser.While you are signed in, each event also carries a code that stands for your account. With it we see how readers with an account use the site over time. The code is not your email address, your name, or the number of your account, and PostHog receives none of those. After you sign out, the events carry no code. The records of searches never carry it.We use this to see which parts of the site help readers, and to find faults.Ask an assistantA paper offers “Ask an assistant”. When you use it, your browser opens the site of the assistant that you chose (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, or Grok) with your question and the paragraphs that it is about. UrantiaHub does not receive the question or the answer, and it pays no assistant. The company of that assistant handles both under its own terms. The site counts that the feature was used, never the words.SearchesWhen you search, the site records the words of the search and how many results it found, so that we can see what readers look for. The record has no link to you. Our server sends it, not your browser, so it holds no IP address, no page that you opened, and nothing about your device.To find results, our server sends the words of the search to the content service that holds the text. For the related passages, that service sends the words to OpenAI, which compares their meaning with the text. Neither one receives your IP address or anything else about you.Request logsAs for each website, our hosting provider keeps a log of each request for a limited time, to keep the site safe and to find faults. A log line holds your IP address and the address of the page. The address of a search page holds the words of the search.If you sign inYou can sign in with a UrantiaHub account. It is optional, and nothing on the site needs it for reading or listening. If you sign in, the account holds these things, so that they are the same on each of your devices:Your email address, and your name if your account has one.The last place that you read.Which paragraphs you read, and when.The paragraphs that you saved.Your notes.One cookie holds your sign-in. It is encrypted, and a script on the page cannot read it. A second cookie says only that you are signed in, so that the page can show the right controls. We use neither one for analytics or advertising. While you are signed in, each analytics event carries a code that stands for your account, as the section on page analytics says.A few more cookies serve the sign-in itself: one holds the start of a sign-in for a few minutes, one remembers that you signed out, so that the next sign-in asks you again, and one says for a minute that a sign-in did not finish. The browser also keeps a mark that says which account the place in this browser belongs to.When you sign out on a device, the last place that you read leaves that browser. Your account still holds it.On accounts.urantiahub.com you can see each app that has access to your account, remove one, or delete the account. When you delete the account, we delete what it holds.If you turn on a noticeA signed-in reader can get a passage each day, one reminder of a place, and a note about a new release. Each one is off until you turn it on, and you choose email, a push, or both. A push goes to each device where you are signed in and allowed notifications. If you turn one on, the account also holds these things:Which notices you turned on, and if each one goes by email, as a push, or both.The hour of the daily passage, and your time zone.For each device where you allowed notifications: its address at the push service of the browser, two keys that encrypt a push for that device, and a short name such as “Chrome on Android”.A record of each notice that we sent you: which notice, for which day, and by email or to a device. We keep it for 90 days, so that no notice goes out two times.An email of a notice loads no picture and has no tracking link, so we do not see if you open it. Each one has a link that turns that email off with one press and no sign-in. The link holds a code that names your account and that notice, and nothing more.A push goes through the push service of your browser: Google for Chrome, Apple for Safari, Mozilla for Firefox. It is encrypted for your device, so that service cannot read it. It sees the address of your device at that service, and the time. Your browser keeps a small program that shows the push. A push shows only while you are signed in on that device. When you sign out on a device, we remove that device from your account, and the browser ends its registration. It comes back when you sign in again there.You can turn each notice off on the Notifications page, and “Turn all off” there removes each device. When you delete the account, we delete all of this.What stays in your browserThe site keeps these things in your browser. If you sign in, the place and the paragraphs that you read also go to your account. The theme and the text size never leave the device. For each other reader, nothing of this leaves the device.Your theme, so the page opens light or dark, as you chose.Your text size, so a paper opens at the size that you chose.The last place that you read, so the contents page can offer to continue there.The paragraph where the voice stopped, so the paper can offer to continue listening there.The last page that you read in each world text, so the page of that work can offer to continue there.The papers, if you turn on Read offline, so you can read with no connection; turn it off, and they leave the device.The assistant that you chose, if you use Ask an assistant, so the next question opens in the same one.If you added the site to your home screen: that it opened in this session, so it opens at your last place only when it starts; the mark goes when the app closes.Your last five searches, so you can run one again; the Clear control on the search page removes them.You can remove each of them when you clear the site data in your browser.Who handles data for usThese companies do work for the site. Each one receives only what its work needs.CompanyWhat it doesWhat it receivesVercelPolicyHosts the site.Each request to the site: your IP address, the page address, the time, and your browser type.PostHogPolicyAnalytics: which pages and features readers use.An event for a page or an action, with your IP address. No cookie. While you are signed in, also a code that stands for your account: not your email address and not your name. Also the search records, which have no link to you.CloudflarePolicyServes the audio files, and runs the content service that holds the text.Your IP address when your browser loads an audio file. The words of a search, from our server.OpenAIPolicyCompares the meaning of a search with the text, for the related passages.The words of a search, from the content service. Nothing about you.SupabasePolicyHolds the accounts, and what an account keeps.Your email address, your name and picture if you sign in with Google, the last place that you read, which paragraphs you read, the paragraphs that you saved, and your notes.ResendPolicySends the email with the sign-in code, and the notices that you chose to get by email.Your email address, and the text of the email.GooglePolicySigns you in, only if you choose “Continue with Google”.That you signed in to UrantiaHub. Google gives us your email address, your name, and your picture.We do not sell data, and we do not share it for advertising.Your choicesIf your browser sends a Do Not Track or a Global Privacy Control signal, the site sends no analytics event and makes no record of your searches in its analytics.You can clear the site data in your browser at any time. The site still works.You can ask what we hold, or ask us to remove it: privacy@urantiahub.com. With no account, we hold nothing under your name, so tell us what you look for.Changes to this pageNew on this date: this page no longer calls saved paragraphs and notes planned, because you can use them now. It also corrects one line about the sign-in cookies: while you are signed in, analytics events carry a code that stands for your account. When this page changes, the date at the top changes, and this page says what is new.ContactSend a question about your privacy to privacy@urantiahub.com."`;

